NAME
       wipefs - list or erase file system, RAID and partition-table signatures

SYNOPSIS
       wipefs [-n] [-q] [-t TYPE] DEVICE
       wipefs -a [-f] DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       Every file system, swap area, LVM physical volume and partition table
       starts with a signature: a few "magic" bytes that tell tools what the
       device holds. Without options, wipefs lists the signatures it finds on
       DEVICE with their offset and type. With -a it erases them, so that
       tools see an empty device again (for example before pvcreate or
       mkfs complains about an old file system). Only the signature bytes are
       erased, not the data, but the old contents become unreachable for
       practical purposes.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
              Erase every signature found.

       -o, --offset OFFSET
              Erase only the signature at this offset.

       -t, --types TYPE
              Only signatures of these types (such as xfs or gpt).

       -n, --no-act
              Show what would be erased without erasing.

       -f, --force
              Erase even if the device is in use.

       -q, --quiet
              Print fewer messages.

EXAMPLES
           wipefs /dev/vdb
           wipefs -a /dev/vdb

NOTES
       Make sure you have the right device: wipefs -a on a disk in use
       destroys access to its data. Unmount it first.

SEE ALSO
       blkid(8), lsblk(8), parted(8), mkfs(8)
